NAME
       findmnt - list mounted file systems, or check /etc/fstab

SYNOPSIS
       findmnt [OPTION...] [MOUNTPOINT | DEVICE]
       findmnt -s
       findmnt --verify

DESCRIPTION
       findmnt shows the mounted file systems as a tree: each mount point,
       its source (device, LV, NFS export), the file system type and the
       mount options. Give a mount point or a device to see just that one.

           TARGET  SOURCE                 FSTYPE OPTIONS
           /data   /dev/mapper/vgdata-lv1 xfs    rw,relatime,seclabel,attr2,...

       It exits with status 1 when nothing matches, so findmnt /data is a
       good test in scripts and checks that something is mounted there.

       findmnt --verify reads /etc/fstab and reports mistakes (a device or
       UUID that does not exist, a missing mount point, an unknown type)
       without mounting anything. Run it after every fstab change, before you
       reboot: a broken fstab line can stop the machine from booting.

OPTIONS
       -s, --fstab
              List the entries of /etc/fstab instead of what is mounted.

       --verify
              Check /etc/fstab. With -v, also show the entries that are fine.

       -t TYPE, --types TYPE
              Only file systems of these types (xfs,ext4,nfs4...).

       -S DEVICE, --source DEVICE
              Only mounts of this source.

       -T PATH, --target PATH
              The file system that holds PATH.

       -M DIR, --mountpoint DIR
              Only this mount point.

       -o LIST, --output LIST
              The columns to print: TARGET, SOURCE, FSTYPE, OPTIONS, UUID,
              LABEL, SIZE, USED, AVAIL, USE%.

       -n, --noheadings
              No header line.

       -l, --list
              A list instead of a tree.

       -r, --raw
              Raw output, columns separated by single spaces.

       -D, --df
              Sizes like df.

       -f, --first-only
              Print only the first match.

       -k, --kernel
              Read the kernel's list (the default).

       -m, --mtab
              Read /etc/mtab.

       --real
              Only real file systems (no proc, sysfs, cgroup...).

EXAMPLES
       Is something mounted on /data, and what?

           findmnt /data

       Only the source and type, for a script:

           findmnt -n -o SOURCE,FSTYPE /data

       All NFS mounts:

           findmnt -t nfs4

       Check /etc/fstab, then mount everything in it:

           findmnt --verify
           mount -a

SEE ALSO
       mount(8), umount(8), fstab(5), lsblk(8), df(1)
